\layout Section

Terminology
\layout Standard

Before we cover these issues in more detail, let's introduce some standard
 terminology.
 Typically a series of communications between two users/computers is known
 as a 
\emph on 
session
\emph default 
.
 The interactions themselves consist of 
\emph on 
messages
\emph default 
 each of which may be split into 
\emph on 
packets
\emph default 
.
 
\layout Standard

One example of a session would be a lecture.
 The session consists of a mainly one-way communication between the lecturer
 (the server) and the students (multiple clients).
 In this case most of the information transfer is one-way with the lecturer
 sending a series of messages, each involving a different subject: the lecturer'
s identity, the course's subject matter 
\emph on 
etc
\emph default 
.
 and you acknowledging by your lively reactions.
 Each message consists of a series of packets, in this case perhaps the
 sentences that the lecturer speaks.
 The communication medium is the air.
 
\layout Section

Session Characteristics
\layout Standard

Depending on the nature of the information communicated between the client
 and the server the characteristics of the session can vary dramatically.
 An FTP session may involve a few packets from the client to request a file
 followed by many packets from the server to send the file.
 A VoIP session will involve large numbers of packets between two users
 and no obvious client/server relationship.
 An hypertext transfer protocol (HTTP) session may involve long periods
 of inactivity as the client reads the pages they have downloaded.
\layout Standard

Each of the following six criteria/characteristics of a session may need
 to be considered in the design of the protocol and the network.
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Message arrival rate
\series default 
.
 We can seek to model the arrivals as: 
\emph on 
Poisson arrivals
\emph default 
, 
\emph on 
deterministic arrivals
\emph default 
, 
\emph on 
uniform arrivals
\emph default 
 or as coming from a more general class of distributions.
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Session holding time
\series default 
: The length of a session.
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Expected message length
\series default 
 and 
\series bold 
length distribution
\series default 
, message lengths can be modelled as 
\emph on 
exponential distributions
\emph default 
,
\emph on 
 uniform length
\emph default 
.
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Allowable delay
\series default 
 - acceptable expected delays vary.
 The acceptable delay is low for VoIP and quite high for FTP.
 
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Reliability
\series default 
 - FTP 
\emph on 
must
\emph default 
 be error free but VoIP can have some bit errors or packet loss without
 major consequences.
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Message and packet ordering
\series default 
 - Some sessions will require that the packets arrive in the order they
 are sent: for example database requests.
\layout Standard

In this course we will be particularly interested in the first five of these
 characteristics.
 
\layout Section

Network Types
\layout Standard

In our review of the history of communication networks, we have already
 met two distinct kinds of network.
 
\layout Description

Circuit\SpecialChar ~
Switching: used for telephone routing.
 Guarantees an open `channel' between two peers.
 
\layout Description

Packet\SpecialChar ~
Switching or Store and Forward Switching: used for data.
 May operate via 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Description

Dynamic\SpecialChar ~
Routing Each packet of the message moves through the network independentl
y.
 
\layout Description

Virtual\SpecialChar ~
Circuit\SpecialChar ~
Routing A fixed path through the network is provided for the
 entire message.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

The character of the two approaches are quite different.
\layout Subsection

Circuit\SpecialChar ~
Switching
\layout Standard

Circuit switching can be wasteful in terms of bandwidth.
 The communicating nodes may not be using all the available bandwidth reserved
 for them all of the time.
 However, once connection is established, a guaranteed channel is provided
 with a known communication delay associated with it.
 
\layout Standard

In a circuit switching network, the sum of rates for all sessions using
 a link cannot exceed the total capacity of the link.
 New sessions must be rejected when the link capacity is reached.
 The approach is used for telephone networks where the session rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{s}$
\end_inset 

, is the same for each session.
 Switching would be more complex for data networks and the nature of transmissio
n would also mean that the links are inefficiently used.
 
\layout Subsection

Packet Switching
\layout Standard

Packet switching makes efficient use of bandwidth because, whilst communication
 between two nodes may be idle for a period of time, packets from another
 communication may be sent along the same line.
 However because it is unknown 
\emph on 
a priori
\emph default 
 how much bandwidth will be available it is difficult to guarantee a maximum
 delay.
 
