\layout Subsection

Notational Details
\layout Standard

Strictly speaking, when we write probability distributions we should write
 them out in full (
\emph on 
e.g.

\emph default 
 for the joint distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X=x,Y=y\right)$
\end_inset 

).
 However, in practice, we often use a shorthand notation: 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 This is convenient, but it can lead to confusion.
 It looks very much like we might write a multivariate function, 
\emph on 
e.g.
 
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x,y\right)=\frac{x}{y}$
\end_inset 

.
 For a multivariate function though, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x,y\right)\neq f\left(y,x\right)$
\end_inset 

 in general.
 However, for our probability shorthand 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(y,x\right)$
\end_inset 

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X=x,Y=y\right)=P\left(Y=y,X=x\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

We have denoted the probability distribution with a capital 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset 

.
 In 
\emph on 
these notes
\emph default 
 we will use capital 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset 

s consistently to denote discrete distributions.
 Later, when we come on to continuous probability density functions, we
 will turn to small letters.
\layout Section

Probability Rules
\layout Standard

From the simple definitions we've given above we can derive some straightforward
 rules associated with manipulating probability distributions.
 
\layout Subsection

Normalisation
\layout Standard

The first point to note is that probability distributions are normalised,
 the sum over the distribution across each of its possible value is 1.
 This is clear from the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{x}n_{X=x}=N$
\end_inset 

, which gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{x}P\left(x\right)=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\sum_{x}n_{X=x}}{N}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{N}{N}=1.\]

\end_inset 

A similar result can be derived for the marginal and conditional distributions.
\layout Subsection

The Sum Rule 
\layout Standard

The sum rule describes how we can obtain a marginal distribution from the
 joint distribution.
 The distribution is called the marginal distribution because one of the
 variables in the joint distribution is
\emph on 
 marginalised
\emph default 
, 
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 it is removed from the distribution (you can think of it being written
 in the margin).
 The marginal probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(y\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{Y=y}}{N}$
\end_inset 

 and the joint distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{X=x,Y=y}}{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 Now we note (using Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:aJointDistribution}

\end_inset 

) that 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{Y=y}=\sum_{x}n_{X=x,Y=y}$
\end_inset 

, where the sum is over all possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 

.
 We therefore find that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{Y=y}}{N}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{x}\frac{n_{X=x,Y=y}}{N},\]

\end_inset 

or in other words
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(y\right)=\sum_{x}P\left(x,y\right).\]

\end_inset 

This is known as the sum rule of probability.
 
\layout Subsection

The Product Rule
\layout Standard

The product rule describes how we obtain a joint distribution from a conditional
 distribution and a marginal.
 Recall that the conditional distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x|y\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{X=x,Y=y}}{n_{Y=y}}$
\end_inset 

.
 This can be related to the joint distribution over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 and the marginal distribution over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 using the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{X=x,Y=y}}{N}=\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n_{X=x,Y=y}}{n_{Y=y}}\frac{n_{Y=y}}{N}\]

\end_inset 

or in other words
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(x|y\right)P\left(y\right).\]

\end_inset 

This is known as the product rule of probability.
\layout Subsection

Bayes' Rule
\layout Standard

Less relevant to our work on queueing theory, but worth pointing out since
 we've come so far is Bayes' Rule or Bayes' Theorem.
 Since the joint distribution is commutative in its arguments we have, from
 the product rule,
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(y,x\right)=P\left(y|x\right)P\left(x\right),\]

\end_inset 

 it naturally follows that we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x|y\right)P\left(y\right)=P\left(y|x\right)P\left(x\right)\]

\end_inset 

which leads to Bayes' Rule, which relates the two conditional probabilities
 associated with the variables:
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x|y\right)=\frac{P\left(y|x\right)P\left(x\right)}{P\left(y\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Independence
\layout Standard

In the course we will often state that two or more variables are assumed
 to be independent.
 Probabilistically independence refers to the affect of the knowledge of
 one variable on the distribution of another.
 Mathematically this means that our probability distribution over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is not affected by the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

, or in other words
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x\right)=P\left(x|y\right),\]

\end_inset 

the conditional distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 is equal to the marginal distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

.
 This in turn implies that the joint distribution factorises,
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(x|y\right)P\left(y\right)=P\left(x\right)P\left(y\right),\]

\end_inset 

and furthermore that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(y|x\right)=P\left(y\right).\]

\end_inset 

The factorisation of the joint distribution when variables are independent
 turns out to be a very important property which makes a lot of analysis
 tractable.
\layout Section

Expectations
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:expectations}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

A probabilistic model of a system may summarise our beliefs about the system,
 but to develop further understandings of the model often want to summarise
 the model by its expectations.
\layout Standard

The expected value of a function, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 

, under a probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(x\right)}=\sum_{x}P\left(x\right)f\left(x\right).\]

\end_inset 

Here we have introduced a particular notation for the expectation, you will
 also see expectations written in the form 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ f\left(x\right)\right\} $
\end_inset 

.
 
