Some values for the Poisson distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=2$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonTable}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

 
\layout Standard

As we mentioned in Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:expectations}

\end_inset 

, the expectations under a distribution are important for summarising the
 distributions characteristics.
 Expectations of distributions that take the form of a function can also
 be computed.
 Typically we expect the resulting expectation to be a function of the distribut
ions parameters.
 In the Examples section at the end of this chapter we compute the mean
 of a Poisson distribution.
\layout Section

Continuous Variables
\layout Standard

So far we have discussed a value for discrete values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

.
 We now turn to a continuous models.
 First though we introduce the probability distribution's sister, the 
\emph on 
probability density function
\emph default 
 (PDF).
 In the discrete case, we defined probability distributions over a discrete
 number of states.
 However, if we wish to model a continuous value, such as the height of
 a computer science student, how do we represent it as a probability distributio
n? One way would be to `discretise' the continuous value by splitting it
 into discrete regions.
 We effectively did this in the example in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:aJointDistribution}

\end_inset 

.
 So, for our height example, we could model the probability that your height
 was between 
\begin_inset Formula $1.6\textrm{m}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1.7\textrm{m}$
\end_inset 

, or alternatively we could ask what is the probability that you are over
 
\begin_inset Formula $1.8\textrm{m}$
\end_inset 

 given that you are a computer scientist? However, remodelling for each
 of these questions would be rather tedious.
 It would be more convenient if we could develop a representation which
 could answer 
\emph on 
any 
\emph default 
question we chose to ask about a computer scientist's height.
 This is what a probability density function represents.
 It does not represent probabilities directly, but from it they may be derived.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/pdfOfHeight.eps
	lyxscale 50
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

A Gaussian PDF representing heights of computer scientists.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:heightPDF}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We represent a probability density function with a small 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\cdot\right)$
\end_inset 

 to differentiate it from a probability distribution which we have denoted
 with a large 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(\cdot\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 We define it to be a positive function whose integral over the region of
 interest is one
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

In what follows we shall use the word distribution to refer to both discrete
 probabilities and continuous probability density functions.
\end_inset 

.
 One example is the Gaussian, shown above to represent a model of a computer
 science student's height.
 We can query the distribution by integrating over regions of it.
 If we wish to ask the question `What is the probability that a computer
 science student is over 
\begin_inset Formula $2\textrm{m}$
\end_inset 

 tall?' The answer, which we denote 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(y>2\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by integrating from 2 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 Since the total area under the curve is always one, and the function is
 always positive, the answer will always be less than 1 and greater than
 0.
 This fulfils our definition of probabilities.
 The answer to the inverse question will be 
\begin_inset Formula $1-P\left(y>2\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Note that this implies that the probability of a student being exactly
 
\begin_inset Formula $2\textrm{m}$
\end_inset 

 tall is zero.
 This may seem odd, but is a consequence of querying the system in the limit
 as a region tends to zero.
 
\layout Subsection

Manipulating PDFs
\layout Standard

The same rules that apply to probability distributions may also be applied
 to PDFs.
 We won't derive this here, but take it as given that
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(y|x\right)=\frac{p\left(x|y\right)p\left(y\right)}{p\left(x\right)}\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x,y\right)=p\left(x|y\right)p\left(y\right)$
\end_inset 

 and for continuous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)=\int p\left(x,y\right)dy$
\end_inset 

.
 Here the integral is over the region for which our PDF for 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 is defined (
\emph on 
e.g.
 
\emph default 
all real numbers or all positive real numbers).
 Additionally we may mix discrete and continuous variables.
 If, for example 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 is discrete and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is continuous, then we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(y|x\right)=\frac{p\left(x|y\right)P\left(y\right)}{p\left(x\right)}.\]

\end_inset 

Finally expectations under a PDF are also computed using the integral instead
 of the sum so 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle _{p\left(x\right)}=\int f\left(x\right)p\left(x\right)dx\]

\end_inset 

where the integral is over the region for which our PDF for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is defined.
\layout Subsection

Cumulative Distribution Functions
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We've discussed how a PDF doesn't represent probabilities directly, but
 it is a mechanism by which questions about the random variable can be answered.
 One very common question is: what is the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $x<y$
\end_inset 

? It can be useful to have a function which stores the answer to this question
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

.
 This function is known as the cumulative distribution function (CDF).
 The CDF is given by the integral of the PDF across all values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 less than 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

.
 So if the probability distribution is valid in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $-\infty<x<\infty$
\end_inset 

 the CDF is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x<y\right)=\int_{-\infty}^{y}p\left(x\right)dx,\]

\end_inset 

alternatively, if the distribution is defined in the range 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq x<\infty$
\end_inset 

 then the CDF is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x<y\right)=\int_{0}^{y}p\left(x\right)dx.\]

\end_inset 

To complete the distribution we need the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $x>y$
\end_inset 

 (which is the other possibility).
 This is easy to compute because we know the distribution must be normalised,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x>y\right)=1-P\left(x<y\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

In Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:heightPDF}

\end_inset 

 we give an example of a cumulative distribution function.
 We show the CDF associated with the PDF of the computer science students'
 height.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/cdfOfHeight.eps
	lyxscale 50
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

The cumulative distribution function (CDF) for the heights of computer science
 students.
 The thick curve gives the CDF and the thinner curve the associated PDF.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Finally, we note that since the cumulative density function is the integral
 of the probability density function the PDF can be recovered from the CDF
 through differentiation.
\layout Section

Sample Based Approximations
\layout Standard

Recall from Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:expectations}

\end_inset 

 that expectation of any given function, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

, under a distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

, can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}=\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

which is also written as 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left\{ f\left(k\right)\right\} $
\end_inset 

.
 Recall our definition of probability from Table\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:aJointDistribution}

\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\frac{s_{k}}{S}\]

\end_inset 

where we have now used 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 (instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

) as the total number of samples or trials from a system and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{k}$
\end_inset 

 is the number that have state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 When we make 
\emph on 
sample based approximations 
\emph default 
we hope that 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 is `large enough
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

How large 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 need be is dependent on both the function whose expectation we are estimating
 and the distribution under which we are taking the expectation.
\end_inset 

' such that it is a good approximation to say
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}\approx\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)\frac{s_{k}}{S}.\]

\end_inset 

This formula may be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)\frac{s_{k}}{S}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)s_{k}.\]

\end_inset 

Now if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset 

 is a vector of samples from 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

with elements 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{i}$
\end_inset 

 and length 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 then we can rewrite the above
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{S}\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)s_{k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(k_{i}\right)\]

\end_inset 

which provides us with a sample based approximation to the true expectation:
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}\approx\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(k_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 

Some well know special cases of this include the 
\emph on 
sample mean, 
\emph default 
often denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{k}$
\end_inset 

, and computed as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}k_{i},\]

\end_inset 

this sample based mean is an approximation to the true distribution mean
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle \approx\bar{k}.\]

\end_inset 

The same approximations can be used in the context of continuous PDFs, so
 we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle _{p\left(x\right)} & = & \int f\left(x\right)p\left(x\right)dx\\
 & \approx & \frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(x_{i}\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset 

 are independently obtained samples from the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

The approximation gets better for increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 and worse if the samples from 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

 are 
\emph on 
not 
\emph default 
independent.
\layout Paragraph

Sample Means for Student Heights
\layout Standard

Returning to our example concerning the height of computer science students.
 If we don't know the true underlying distribution of their heights, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(h\right)$
\end_inset 

, we cannot compute the mean of the distribution, but we can approximate
 it with the sample mean: so if we want the mean, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(h\right)=h$
\end_inset 

, we use
\begin_inset Formula \[
\int_{0}^{\infty}hp\left(h\right)dh\approx\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(k_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Paragraph

Sample and Distribution Means
\layout Standard

In most texts the word mean is used interchangeably to describe sample based
 means and the true mean of a distribution.
 However, you should always bear in mind the important difference between
 the two.
 Later in the course we will also come across time based averages and assumption
s will be made that the time based average is equal to the average under
 a `steady state distribution'.
\layout Subsection

Mean and Variance
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonMeanVar}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We compute the mean of a Poisson in the following example.
 The variance is an exercise in the first tutorial sheet.
\layout Section*

Examples
\layout Enumerate

Compute the mean of the Poisson distribution, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
the expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 under the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k|\mu\right)=\frac{\mu^{k}}{k!}e^{-\mu}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The mean is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{P\left(k|\mu\right)}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\frac{\mu^{k}}{k!}e^{-\mu}\]

\end_inset 

 To make progress with this we first pull out 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu e^{-\mu}$
\end_inset 

 and the first 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 in the factorial, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{k}{k!}=\frac{k}{k(k-1)!}=\frac{1}{(k-1)!}$
\end_inset 

.
 We can also remove the first term from the sum because it is multiplied
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 which is zero for the first term.
\layout Standard

This leads to
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =e^{-\mu}\mu\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{\mu^{(k-1)}}{(k-1)!}\]

\end_inset 

 Now we make the following substitution: 
\begin_inset Formula $m=k-1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\langle k\right\rangle =e^{-\mu}\mu\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mu^{m}}{m!}\label{eq:poissonMean2}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 We now make use of the following geometric series,
\begin_inset Formula \[
e^{\mu}=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{\mu^{m}}{m!}\]

\end_inset 

 Substituting this result back into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:poissonMean2}

\end_inset 

) we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\mu\label{eq:poissonMean3}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


