\layout Chapter

Poisson Processes
\layout Standard

In Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:distributionAsFunction}

\end_inset 

 we introduced probability distributions whose values were given by functions.
 In this chapter we will introduce probabilistic processes.
 
\layout Section

Stochastic Processes
\layout Standard

A probabilistic process, also known as a random process or a stochastic
 process, can be viewed as a `random function'.
 A normal function maps a set of inputs to a point value.
 A probabilistic process maps a set of inputs to a probability distribution
 over values or a probability density function.
\layout Standard

Various systems are modelled with probabilistic processes.
 For example, the 
\emph on 
Wiener process
\emph default 
 is the process behind Brownian motion.
 Stock market data is often modelled as a stochastic process.
 For example the Black-Scholes formula assumes that the logarithm of the
 stock market data can be modelled as a Wiener Process.
\layout Standard

We will base our definition of a random process on that given by 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Chan:performance00}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Description

A\SpecialChar ~
random\SpecialChar ~
process May be defined as a 
\emph on 
random function
\emph default 
 which, for a particular value, is a random quantity.
 
\layout Section

Poisson Process
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:poissonProcess}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We've already met the Poisson distribution, there is a simple process which
 is related to the Poisson distribution: the Poisson process.
\layout Standard

The Poisson process gives probabilities of a number of arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, as a function of time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, given a `rate', 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|t,\lambda\right)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eq:process}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

Note the similarity between (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:poissonDist}

\end_inset 

) and (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

).
 The process is simply the distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 substituted with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Using this substitution the mean of the Poisson process (the first moment
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

) is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\lambda t.\]

\end_inset 

Which is the expected number of arrivals at a given time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is therefore known as the 
\emph on 
rate
\emph default 
 of the process.
 This implies that the Poisson process is a form of counting process where
 the counts are 
\emph on 
expected
\emph default 
 to increase linearly with time at a rate given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Section

Properties
\layout Standard

In the Chapter\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cha:markovChainsAndProcesses}

\end_inset 

 we will derive the Poisson process from some simple assumptions about how
 arrivals occur, for the moment though we will highlight some important
 properties of the Poisson process.
 We will describe each in more detail below, to summarise though the fiver
 properties are:
\layout Enumerate

Superposition of Poisson processes.
\layout Enumerate

Decomposition of Poisson processes.
\layout Enumerate

Independence of arrivals.
\layout Enumerate

Similarity of arrival distribution.
\layout Enumerate

Interarrival time distributions.
\layout Subsection

Superposition of Poissons
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:superpositionPoisson}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The first property we will consider is superposition of Poisson flows.
 It is a property of a Poisson process that if we combine 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 independent
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

Two variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset 

 are independent if their joint distributions are equal to the product of
 their marginal distributions 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x,y\right)=P\left(x\right)P\left(y\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 

 processes with rates 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\dots,\lambda_{N}$
\end_inset 

 together the resulting process is also a Poisson process with a combined
 rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, equal to the sum of each of the input rates: 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown pictorially in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:poissonSuperposition}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/sumPoisson.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

If independent Poisson processes form a single traffic flow overall process
 is a Poisson with a rate that s the sum of the rates of the separate processes.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonSuperposition}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Decomposition of Poisson Flow
\layout Standard

Just as Poisson processes can be combined to obtain a higher rate Poisson
 process, they can also be decomposed to obtain lower rate Poisson processes.
 Assume we have one input channel where arrivals are occurring with a Poisson
 process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 This channel then splits into 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 separate output channels.
 Each input arrival is then 
\emph on 
independently
\emph default 
 assigned to output channel 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 In this case the output channels then behave like Poisson processes with
 rates given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{n}=P\left(n\right)\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:decompositionPoisson}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/decomposePoisson.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

If a single Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is switched to different output channels, with each arrival being switched
 to channel 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(n\right)=\lambda_{n}/\lambda$
\end_inset 

, each output channel will be a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:decompositionPoisson}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Independence of Arrivals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonIndependence}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process, if we look within this process at two
 
\emph on 
disjoint
\emph default 
 time intervals (
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 periods of time that don't overlap).
 Assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the first interval and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the second interval.
 The distribution of arrivals in the two intervals are independent of each
 other, 
\emph on 
i.e.\SpecialChar ~

\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k_{1},k_{2}\right)=P\left(k_{1}\right)P\left(k_{2}\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Subsection

Similarity of Arrival Distributions for Identical Intervals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

.
 Now consider a second independent Poisson process with the same rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 The distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 are then equal, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k_{1}\right)=P\left(k_{2}\right).\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Subsection

Interarrival Times and Poisson Processes
\layout Standard

The Poisson process is a simple model of arrivals that is widely used due
 to its analytical properties.
 We have already seen how a superpositions and decompositions of Poisson
 processes also lead to Poisson processes.
 Another attractive analytical property is the ease with which we can compute
 the 
\emph on 
interarrival time distribution
\emph default 
 for the Poisson process.
 
\layout Standard

The interarrival time distribution is the distribution of times between
 arrivals.
 Consider waiting for the next bus at a bus stop.
 If you 
\emph on 
just missed a bus
\emph default 
 how long will you have to wait for the next bus to arrive? If the buses
 arrive as a Poisson process, then it is straightforward to work out the
 distribution of your waiting time.
\layout Standard

The interarrival time distribution for the Poisson process turns out to
 be the 
\emph on 
exponential distribution
\emph default 
,
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(X\right)=\lambda e^{-\lambda X}.\]

\end_inset 

 This is derived in the examples at the end of this chapter.
\layout Standard

Stating that interarrival times are distributed exponentially is equivalent
 to stating that arrivals occur as a Poisson process.
 We will therefore use these formulations interchangeably, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
when we state that the interarrival times for students registering at the
 Goodwin Sports centre is given by an exponential distribution with parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

 we are equivalently stating that these students are arriving according
 to a Poisson process with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Section

The Exponential Distribution
\layout Standard

In the last section we saw that the exponential distribution gives can be
 derived by considering the interarrival times of the Poisson distribution.
 Here we give a few properties of the exponential that will be relevant
 in this course.
\layout Subsection

The Memoryless property
\layout Standard

We have already seen in our derivation of the exponential as interarrival
 time distribution that it is 
\emph on 
memoryless
\emph default 
.
 Formally speaking this means that for exponentially distributed variable
 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X>s+t|X>t\right)=P\left(X>s\right)\]

\end_inset 

or in English, the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 given that the waiting time is already greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is the same as the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsection

Mean and Variance
\layout Standard

Recall the exponential distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(X)=\lambda e^{-\lambda X}\]

\end_inset 

 The mean of an exponential can be found to be, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X\right\rangle _{p(X)}=\frac{1}{\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle _{p(X)}-\left\langle X\right\rangle _{p(X)}^{2}=\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

 These are easily confirmed --- they are part of Tutorial 1.
 
