\layout Chapter

Markov Chains and Markov Processes
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cha:markovChainsAndProcesses}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the last chapter we introduced the Poisson process.
 In this chapter we will show how the Poisson process in a special case
 of the more general class of processes known as Markov processes.
 We will start by considering Markov processes as they operate in 
\emph on 
discrete time
\emph default 
, otherwise known as 
\emph on 
Markov chains
\emph default 
.
 Both Markov processes and Markov chains exhibit the 
\emph on 
Markov property 
\emph default 
which is an assumption about how future values drawn from the process depend
 on past values.
 We will first discuss the Markov property in the context of Markov chains,
 then we will show how a discrete system can be converted to a continuous
 system.
\layout Section

Markov Chains
\layout Standard

Time is a continuous variable, but sometimes it is convenient, or natural,
 to discretise time.
 One example of a form of discretised time would be turns in a board game.
 As the turns progress this discretised form of time progresses.
\layout Standard

Consider a discrete representation of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,1,2...$
\end_inset 

.
 We associate a state variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

, with this time such that we have values for 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0),\, N(1),\, N(2),\,...$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Subsection

Markov Property
\layout Standard

If this state variable exhibits the Markov property its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is dependent only on its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 The common way of putting this property is that the future is conditional
 independent of the past.
 The conditioning being on the present.
 In other words, the state of the system in the future is 
\emph on 
not 
\emph default 
dependent on what happened in the past if we know the state at the present
 time.
\layout Standard

Let's consider a real-life example of a Markov chain, that of the board
 game of Monopoly.
\layout Subsection

Monopoly Board 
\layout Standard

There are forty squares on a Monopoly board.
 We assume that there is only one player.
 That player's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

, will be the number of the square his/her counter is on 
\begin_inset Formula $+40$
\end_inset 

 for every time he/she has reached GO.
 For example if you have circled the board twice and are currently on GO
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=80$
\end_inset 

.
 Or if you have circled the board once and are on King's Cross then 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=45$
\end_inset 

.
 The time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is now clearly discrete and associated with the number of turns the player
 has had.
 
\layout Standard

In Monopoly you move your counter through rolling two six sided dice and
 adding them together.
 We will denote the result of this die roll at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

.
 Notice that your state after turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

 depends only on your die role, 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

, and your state before turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t-1)$
\end_inset 

.
 We write this as: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
N(t)=N(t-1)+d(t).\]

\end_inset 

 This means that Monopoly exhibits the Markov property, as your future states
 are independent of your previous states given your current state.
\layout Subsection

State Diagrams
\layout Standard

A Markov chain can be represented by a state diagram just as finite state
 machines are often represented.
 One important difference is that Markov chains are not necessarily finite,
 they can have infinite states! A further difference is that the transitions
 for Markov chains are associated with probabilities (or in the case of
 Markov processes rates).
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/smallMonopoly.jpg

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Image of a monopoly board.
\end_inset 


\layout Section

Poisson Process as a Markov Process
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:poissonAsMarkov}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We've introduced the game of Monopoly as a Markov chain.
 In this next session we shall relate Markov chains, where time is treated
 as a discrete variable, to Markov processes, where time is a continuous
 variable.
 We will show how the Poisson process is a special case of a Markov process
 where a constrained set of transitions are allowed.
 To do this we will first introduce a new version of Monopoly that we call
 `Coin Monopoly'.
 
\layout Subsection

Coin Monopoly
\layout Standard

In regular Monopoly each jump, 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

, is given by the sum of two die rolls.
 Let's assume that we a Monopoly addict Anne who has lost the dice for her
 set of Monopoly.
 In danger of going into withdrawal and, finding a coin in her pocket, she
 comes up with the following way of playing the game.
\layout Standard

In the coin version of Monopoly each player tosses the coin when it is their
 turn.
 Then, instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

 being the sum of two die roles, we take 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)=0$
\end_inset 

 if a coin is tails and 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)=1$
\end_inset 

 if it is heads.
 This is depicted as a state diagram in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:coinMonopolyStateDiagram}

\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

There is a drawback to playing `Coin Monopoly': it takes rather a long time.
 However, Anne is a keen mathematician and tries to find a way of speeding
 it up.
 
\layout Standard

Currently, with each turn, we consider time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, to change by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=1$
\end_inset 

.
 Let's pretend we have a special coin where we can change the probability
 that it will be heads.
 We denote this probability, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(h\right)=\lambda\,\Delta t$
\end_inset 

 (which must be less than 1), giving 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(t\right)=1-\lambda\,\,\Delta t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
placement h
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/stateDiagram.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

State diagram for coin monopoly.
 Each node (circle) represents a different possible state value.
 The state changes (or doesn't change) with each time step according the
 probabilities associated with the arrows.
 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:coinMonopolyStateDiagram}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Anne can compute the probability that, after 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 turns, the state of the system will be 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 using the 
\emph on 
binomial distribution
\emph default 
.
\layout Subsection

Binomial Distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 Trials
\layout Standard

Consider a system with two states (such as a coin).
 Let's assume that one of those states is associated with `success' and
 one is associated with `failure'.
 In our Coin Monopoly example heads might be considered success (because
 it is associated with a move forward) and tails might be associated with
 failure (because it is associated with no move).
 This distribution is known as the binomial.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 trials (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 coin tosses or 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 turns for Coin Monopoly) the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 successes (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 moves forward) is given by 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)P\left(h\right)^{k}(1-P\left(h\right))^{n-k}\label{eq:binomialDistirbution}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)=\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$
\end_inset 

 is known as the binomial coefficient.
 In the examples at the end of this chapter we show that as the number of
 trials, 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, is taken to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

 and the probability of success is driven to zero, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(h\right)=\frac{\lambda}{n}$
\end_inset 

, this system becomes a Poisson process.
 Inserting these two limits into the equation gives: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|\lambda,t\right)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eqn:poisson}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 In other words, we have proved that a Poisson process is a particular type
 of Markov chain, in fact, taking the number of trials to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

 is equivalent to taking the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

, this system is now continuous time, it is therefore known as a Markov
 process rather than a Markov chain.
 A Poisson process is therefore also a particular instance of a Markov process.
\layout Subsection

Markov Process State Diagrams
\layout Standard

When drawing state diagrams for continuous systems there a couple of small
 differences to the Markov chain state diagram given in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:coinMonopolyStateDiagram}

\end_inset 

.
 We no longer include the arrows that point to the same state and we replace
 the probability of state transfer with a rate of transfer between states
 (see Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:coinMonopolyProcess}

\end_inset 

).
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/stateDiagramProcess.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

State diagram for the Markov process given by Coin Monopoly in the limit
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

.
 This is the state diagram for a Poisson process.
 Notice in comparison to Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:coinMonopolyStateDiagram}

\end_inset 

, the probabilities have been replaced by rates and there are no self-transition
 arrows.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:coinMonopolyProcess}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

You've now seen that a Poisson process can be derived starting from a simple
 Markov chain.
 This gives some deeper insight to a Poisson process.
 In the coin monopoly model there is a fixed (constant) probability of moving
 forward in every turn and whether a move forward occurs in each turn is
 completely independent of what happened in previous turns.
 The Poisson process is the continuous analogue of this stem.
 There is a constant probability of an arrival occurring at any time which
 is independent of what has gone before.
 This gives you an intuitive explanation of the two properties of the Poisson
 process given in Sections\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonIndependence}

\end_inset 

\SpecialChar ~
and\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Section*

Examples
\layout Enumerate

It is possible to compute a probability distribution for a number of successes
 given a number of trials.
 This distribution is known as the binomial.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 trials (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 coin tosses or 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 turns for Coin Monopoly) the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 successes (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 moves forward) is given by 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)P\left(h\right)^{k}(1-P\left(h\right))^{n-k}\label{eq:binomialDistirbution}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)=\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$
\end_inset 

 is known as the binomial coefficient.
 
\layout Standard

By considering the limiting case as the probability of a success (or heads)
 goes to zero but the number of trials goes to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

 show that a Poisson process can be recovered.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

Since each of our turns takes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset 

 time, we know that after 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 time we will have completed 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
n=\frac{t}{\Delta t}\label{eq:nInTermsOfDeltaT}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 turns.
 Since we defined 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(h\right)=\lambda\Delta t$
\end_inset 

 we can therefore write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(h\right)=\frac{\lambda t}{n}.\]

\end_inset 

We now substitute this expression for the probability of heads into the
 binomial distribution (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:binomialDistirbution}

\end_inset 

) giving 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k|\lambda,t\right) & = & \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\left(\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{k}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{n-k}\\
 & = & \frac{n\left(n-1\right)\dots\left(n-\left(k-1\right)\right)}{n^{k}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{k}}\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{n}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

The next step is to take the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 From (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:nInTermsOfDeltaT}

\end_inset 

) we can see that this is equivalent to taking 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

.
 In this limit we can take advantage of the following 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{n\left(n-1\right)\dots\left(n-\left(k-1\right)\right)}{n^{k}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{k}}=1.\]

\end_inset 

You should recognise this from the fact that the dominant term in the numerator
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{k}$
\end_inset 

 and the dominant term in the denominator is 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{k}$
\end_inset 

.
 Secondly we use the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{m\rightarrow\infty}\left(1+\frac{z}{m}\right)^{m}=\exp\left(z\right).\]

\end_inset 

Inserting these two limits into the equation gives: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|\lambda,t\right)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eqn:poisson}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

which is recognised as a Poisson process.
 In other words, we have proved that a Poisson process is a particular type
 of Markov chain, in fact, because we have taken the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

, this system is now continuous time, it is therefore known as a Markov
 process.
 
