\layout Section

Global Balance
\layout Standard

We seek a stationary distribution by looking for global balance equations.
 Global balance equations state that at equilibrium the frequency of transitions
 out of state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 equals the frequency of transitions into 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 (pg 260-262 of 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

).
 
\layout Subsection

Discrete Time Markov Chain
\layout Standard

In its most general form a Markov chain can be defined by a transition probabili
ty matrix, this gives the probability of transition from any state to any
 other.
 If the probability of transition from state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 then the transition probability matrix is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{P}=\left[\begin{array}{ccccc}
P_{11} & \cdots & P_{i1} & \cdots & P_{N1}\\
\vdots & \ddots &  &  & \vdots\\
P_{1j} &  & P_{ij} &  & P_{Nj}\\
\vdots &  &  & \ddots & \vdots\\
P_{1N} & \cdots & P_{iN} & \cdots & P_{NN}\end{array}\right].\]

\end_inset 

Under global balance, if the steady state probability of being in state
 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(j\right)$
\end_inset 

, then we must have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P_{ji}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P\left(i\right)P_{ij}\]

\end_inset 

which in English says the probability of leaving state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

 should be equal to the probability of arriving in state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

.
 If this is true then the system is in global balance and there is an associated
 steady state distribution.
\layout Subsection

Continuous Time Markov Process
\layout Standard

In Markov processes we are interested in rates of transfer rather than probabili
ties if the rates of transfer between states are given by a matrix whose
 elements 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{ij}$
\end_inset 

 give the rate of transfer between state 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset 

, then the global balance equations give
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(j\right)\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}q_{ji}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}P\left(i\right)q_{ij}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Section

Detailed Balance
\layout Standard

For the birth death processes we are considering transitions only occur
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k+1$
\end_inset 

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

.
 In other words 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ij}=0$
\end_inset 

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $i>j+1$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $i<j-1$
\end_inset 

.
 This leads us to a special case of the global balance equations known as
 the detailed balance equations which look at the flow between two states.
 For the discrete case
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)P_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)P_{k,k-1}\]

\end_inset 

and for the continuous case 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)q_{k-1,k}=P\left(k\right)q_{k,k-1}.\]

\end_inset 

In our notation rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset 

 and the rate from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k-1}$
\end_inset 

so we have
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k-1\right)\lambda_{k-1}=P\left(k\right)\mu_{k}.\]

\end_inset 

 To understand what this represents it is easier to consider the flow rates
 at a 
\emph on 
boundary 
\emph default 
between two states, 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:flowAtBoundary}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlow.eps
	width 40text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a Boundary for a birth-death process.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundary}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

At steady state, flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 is given 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 For flow to be balanced we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)=\mu_{k}P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda_{k-1}}{\mu_{k}}P\left(k-1\right).\]

\end_inset 

For different systems, the birth rate and death rate will differ.
 We will first study the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue which is a birth-death process where the birth rate and death rate
 are constant regardless of the state of the queue.
\layout Section


\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 Queue
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sec:MM1Stationary}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

From Kendall's notation (Section\SpecialChar ~

