\layout Standard

Having drawn the state diagram the next step is to analyse the flow rates
 at a boundary.
 We analyse between 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlowMM1.eps
	width 40text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a boundary.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundary}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

At steady state, the expected flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Flow from 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $k-1$
\end_inset 

 is given 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu P\left(k-1\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 For flow to be balanced we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P\left(k-1\right)=\mu P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

 leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}P\left(k-1\right).\]

\end_inset 

 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}P\left(0\right)\\
 & = & \rho^{k}P\left(0\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 where we have defined 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, which for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue will turn out to be a useful ratio called the 
\emph on 
utilisation factor
\emph default 
.
 
\layout Subsection

Normalisation
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:normalisationMM1}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

To find 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 we exploit our knowledge that, for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq k<\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 Given that the steady state distribution 
\emph on 
must 
\emph default 
be normalised, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1\]

\end_inset 

 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \[
1=P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}\]

\end_inset 

 giving 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(0\right)=\frac{1}{\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}}.\label{eq:MM1Series}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 We are once again faced with an infinite series in the denominator of (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:MM1Series}

\end_inset 

).
 This series converges if 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho<1$
\end_inset 

 (when the birth rate is less than the death rate), 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\rho^{k}=\frac{1}{1-\rho}\,\,\,\textrm{for\,\,\,$\rho<1$},\]

\end_inset 

 meaning that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(0\right)=\left(1-\rho\right).\label{eq:MM1emptyProb}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 We can now write down the full distribution for the 
\emph on 
steady state
\emph default 
 probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\rho^{k}\left(1-\rho\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Faster Arrivals than Service
\layout Standard

Notice that if the arrival rate (birth rate) is greater than the service
 rate (death rate) then 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho>1$
\end_inset 

 and the series in (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:MM1Series}

\end_inset 

) doesn't converge.
 This is indicative of the fact that if the birth rate is greater than the
 death rate there is no steady state distribution: the queue simply continues
 to grow in size.
\layout Subsection

Average Number in System
\layout Standard

For network design we might be interested in the average queue length.
 We can use the steady state probabilities to compute this quantity.
 
\layout Standard

The average number of customers in the system 
\emph on 
is not
\emph default 
 the same as the average queue length because a customer being served (
\emph on 
e.g.
 
\emph default 
a packet under transmission) is not in the queue.
\layout Standard

The average number of customers in the system is given by the expected state
 of the system,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kP\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

in the examples section at the end of this chapter we show how this average
 can be computed as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)} & = & \frac{\rho}{1-\rho}\\
 & = & \frac{\lambda}{\mu-\lambda}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

The variance can also be computed, it is given by
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\textrm{var}\left[k\right]=\left\langle k^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle k\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

Computation of the variance is an exercise in Tutorial Sheet 2.
 
\layout Standard

As an exercise on the tutorial sheet, you will show that the variance of
 the number in the system is given by
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\textrm{var}\left[k\right]=\left\langle k^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle k\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

 
