\layout Chapter

Little's Formula
\layout Standard

In the last chapter we introduced birth-death processes and demonstrated
 how we could compute the average number in the system for the stationary
 distribution.
 An important quality of service issue is the average delay associated with
 the system.
 We would also like to be able to compute the average number in the queue
 and the average time spent queueing (as opposed to queueing and being served).
\layout Standard

In this chapter we introduce Little's formula, a very general formula which
 can be applied to 
\begin_inset Formula $G/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queues.
 It gives the relationship between the steady-state average number of customers
 in the system, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

, the steady-state arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, and the steady-state customer delay, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}$
\end_inset 

.
 First of all we will introduce a new type of average, the time based average,
 then we introduce Little's formula and review a simple derivation of the
 formula which highlights the conditions under which it applies.
 
\layout Section

Time and Steady State Averages
\layout Standard

We have already introduced the concept of distribution means and sample
 based means.
 In this section we introduce a further type of average, a time based average.
 The time average is the average value of a particular function over time.
 If that function is continuous it is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{f}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}f\left(\tau\right)d\tau.\]

\end_inset 

The derivation of Little's formula that follows will rely on an assumption.
 We will assume that averages under the steady state distribution are equivalent
 to the time average in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, where if the state of the system is given as a function of time as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 the average number in the system as a function of time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau\]

\end_inset 

and the time average after in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\bar{N}\left(t\right).\]

\end_inset 

which we assume is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}\equiv\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 See pg 156-157 in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

 for more details.
 
\layout Section

The Formula
\layout Standard

Little's formula applies under steady state conditions (see Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:steadyState}

\end_inset 

).
 Under the steady state assumptions it states,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}.\]

\end_inset 

In practice we can see what this means by considering a simple example of
 a nightclub queue.
\layout Subsubsection*

Question
\layout Standard

You are driving past a nightclub, wondering how long you will have to wait
 to go in.
 You notice on Thursday nights that the queue typically contains 20 people
 and that people arrive at a rate of 40 per hour.
 
\layout Standard

According to Little's formula, how long would you expect to have to wait
 to get in? 
\layout Subsubsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The waiting time is given by Little's formula.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=20$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=40$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}=\frac{\bar{N}}{\lambda}=\frac{20}{40}=\frac{1}{2}\textrm{hour}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Subsection

Intuition
\layout Standard

When phrased in these terms the formula seems fairly obvious.
 If we see there are twenty people in a queue and they enter a building
 at a rate of 40 per hour, we might expect to have to wait half-an-hour
 in the queue.
 In the examples section at the end of this chapter we build on this intuition
 with a simple graphical `proof' of the formula based upon that given in
 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

The formula applies regardless of the arrival and service time distributions,
 it only relies on the system being in steady state.
 Little's formula can be used at the level of the entire system (queueing
 time and service time), as we have seen, or it may be applied at a lower
 level.
 
\layout Section

Average Delay in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the last chapter we computed that the average state of an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue was given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu-\lambda}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}.\]

\end_inset 

We can use Little's formula to tell us the average delay associated with
 the system.
 From 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}$
\end_inset 

 we rearrange to recover the average delay as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

 This is not the time spent in the queue however, this is the time spent
 in the system which includes 
\emph on 
propagation delay
\emph default 
, the delay in transmitting the packet.
 
\layout Subsection

Little's Formula for Queueing Time and Transmission Time 
\layout Standard

The total delay for packet switching systems can be divided into queueing
 time, 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset 

, and service time or transmission time, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T=X+W.$
\end_inset 

 Similarly the average delay may be decomposed as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 

 Furthermore, both the queue itself and the transmission may the be analysed
 independently using Little's formula.
 
\layout Subsection

The Transmission
\layout Standard

If we assume that customers/packets do not leave the queue then the rate
 of arrivals at the queue must equal the rate of arrivals at transmission.
 If we define the the average number under transmission as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

 then we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\lambda\bar{X},\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 is the average time spend under transmission for each packet.
 For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue the average transmission time is given by the mean of an exponential
 distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, so for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu},\]

\end_inset 

 a formula which we have already used in the last chapter.
 
\layout Standard

The variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

 is known as the utilisation factor.
 It represents the fraction of time that the transmission line (or server)
 is in use and is therefore a measure of efficiency.
 Note that it is also the steady state probability that the transmission
 line is in use.
 For a lossless system (one that doesn't drop packets) this probability
 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $1-P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 You can check that this is consistent for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 by looking at equation (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:MM1emptyProb}

\end_inset 

).
\layout Standard


\emph on 
Note
\emph default 
: time under transmission is often considered proportional to packet length.
 For packet length of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset 

 bits and bandwidth of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset 

 bits per second transmission time is 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\frac{L}{C}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Subsection

The Queue
\layout Standard

Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, an arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

, the average number of packets waiting in the queue, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}$
\end_inset 

, the average packet waiting time, Little's formula gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

for the number in the queue.
 For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\bar{W}=\frac{1}{\mu}+\bar{W}$
\end_inset 

 so the average time spent waiting in the queue is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{T}-\frac{1}{\mu}=\frac{\rho}{\mu-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset 

We can therefore recover the average number in the queue
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\frac{\rho^{2}}{1-\rho}.\]

\end_inset 

 
\layout Section*

Examples
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Graphical Proof of Little's Formula.
 
\series default 
In this example we provide a graphical proof of Little's formula.
\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Subsubsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

First we sketch two curves, the number of arrivals (
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\left(\tau\right)$
\end_inset 

) and the number of departures (
\begin_inset Formula $\beta\left(\tau\right)$
\end_inset 

) from a queue vs time.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/littlesFormula.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Graphical `proof' of Little's formula.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:littlesGraphical}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

By looking at Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:littlesGraphical}

\end_inset 

 we note that the area between the lines in the top figure can be expressed
 as both 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{t}N(\tau)d\tau$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha\left(t\right)}T_{i}$
\end_inset 

.
 We may therefore equate these expressions,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau=\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha(t)}T_{i},\]

\end_inset 

we then introduce the number of arrivals on the right hand side,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau=\alpha\left(t\right)\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha\left(t\right)}T_{i}}{\alpha\left(t\right)}\]

\end_inset 

and divide both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau=\frac{\alpha\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha\left(t\right)}T_{i}}{\alpha\left(t\right)}.\]

\end_inset 

The equation now has the form of three averages.
 The term on the left hand side of the equation is an average, over time,
 of the number in the system.
 This average is a function of time and we write it 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right).\]

\end_inset 

We also note that the right hand side of the equation is also composed of
 two averages: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{\lambda}_{t}=\frac{\alpha\left(t\right)}{t},\]

\end_inset 

is the average of the arrivals over time and 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}_{t}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\alpha\left(t\right)}T_{i}}{\alpha\left(t\right)}\]

\end_inset 

 is the average waiting time.
 So we write
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\bar{\lambda}_{t}\bar{T}_{t}.\]

\end_inset 

Dividing both expressions by 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, equating them, and taking the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 gives Little's formula.
 We wish to consider the steady state behaviour.
 The system may not have steady state behaviour.
 If the steady state exists, we can seek it in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, giving 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}\]

\end_inset 


