\layout Chapter

Other Markov Queues
\layout Standard

We have already met the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue, the simplest Markov queue.
 In this chapter we will introduce related Markov queues and compute their
 stationary distributions.
 The simplest modifications to the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue are given by a 
\emph on 
finite length 
\emph default 
modification which we write in Kendall's notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 and the 
\emph on 
infinite server 
\emph default 
queue
\emph on 
, 
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Section

Finite Length 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In our analysis of the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue so far we have assumed that the queue can have an infinite length,
 in practice a restricted buffer size may mean that the queue length is
 also restricted.
 Let's assume the queue can contain at maximum 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 customers (as shown in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:stateDiagramMM1m}

\end_inset 

), in the Example\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:otherQueuesStationary}

\end_inset 

 at the end of this chapter we derive the stationary distribution for this
 queue.
 In Example\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:otherQueuesBlocking}

\end_inset 

 we introduce the concept of blocking probability.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 queue has limited capacity, that means it is a queue with `loss'.
 The carried load is 
\emph on 
not the same
\emph default 
 as the offered load because customers are rejected when the queue is full.
 These customers are assumed not to return.
\layout Section

Infinite Serving Capacity --- 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue we assume that there is only one server.
 In some cases it can be useful to consider an unlimited number of servers
 so that there is one server for every customer in the system.
 The state diagram for this system can be drawn as in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:stateDiagramMMinf}

\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/stateMMinf.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

State diagram for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:stateDiagramMMinf}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

 
\layout Subsection

Detailed Balance 
\layout Standard

We again make use of the standard detailed balance equations for this queue.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlowMMinf.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a boundary for 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMinf}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

From the flow rates at the boundary we have, in general,
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k-1}P\left(k-1\right)=\mu_{k}P\left(k\right).\]

\end_inset 

Since there is infinite service capacity in the queue the service rate at
 state 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=k\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 The rate of arrivals stays constant, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{k}=\lambda$
\end_inset 

, so for this queue detailed balance gives.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P\left(k-1\right)=k\mu P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

Reorganising in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

 then gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and therefore
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=P\left(0\right)\left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Normalisation Condition
\layout Standard

Using the normalisation condition for this queue, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)=1,\]

\end_inset 

we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\right)^{k}\frac{1}{k!}\right]^{-1}.\label{eq:MMinftyP0}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

The Offered Load
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:offeredLoad}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

It is convenient in this (and later queues) to define the offered load,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, as arrival rate divided by service rate.
 This is a dimensionless constant, but has international standard units
 of Erlangs (after A.\SpecialChar ~
K.\SpecialChar ~
Erlang).
 Substituting this definition back into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:MMinftyP0}

\end_inset 

) we have, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\right]^{-1}.\]

\end_inset 

Recalling from Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonMeanVar}

\end_inset 

 that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n}}{n!}=\exp\left(x\right)\]

\end_inset 

 we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\exp\left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the steady state distribution is then
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{k!}\exp\left(-a\right)\]

\end_inset 

which is recognised as a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

.
 Notice that the utilization factor for this queue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

, is 
\emph on 
not
\emph default 
 equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 which is referred to as the offered load.
 In fact for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue the offered load can be much larger than 1 but we know that the utilizati
on factor is never greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset 

.
 To find the utilization factor for this queue recall that the utilization
 factor is the probability that the queue is busy, which is one minus the
 probability that the queue is empty,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=1-P\left(0\right),\]

\end_inset 

The utilization factor for this queue can be seen to be 
\begin_inset Formula \[
1-P\left(0\right)=1-\exp\left(-a\right).\]

\end_inset 


