\layout Chapter

Erlang Delay 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset 

/
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 
\layout Standard

In the last chapter we considered the situation were there are as many servers
 as there are customers.
 More realistic real world situations involve limited numbers of servers.
 This commonly arises in, for example, call centres.
 This system is known as the 
\emph on 
Erlang delay
\emph default 
 system.
 In Kendall's notation it is written 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Section

Stationary Distribution
\layout Standard

In the context of the Erlang delay system it is usual to refer to the `average
 holding time', 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=\frac{1}{\mu}\equiv\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

, which, for a network involving telephones, would be the time a call is
 open.
\layout Standard

We give a schematic overview of an Erlang delay system in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:erlangDelaySchematic}

\end_inset 

.
 It shows the offered load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, arriving in the system through a queue.
 The system is 
\emph on 
lossless
\emph default 
, so the carried load (sometimes denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}$
\end_inset 

) is also equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\size small 

\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/erlangDelayDiagram.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Queueing model of the Erlang delay system.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:erlangDelaySchematic}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

State Diagram
\layout Standard

As with all the Markov based systems we have met so far the first step in
 their analysis is to write down their state diagram.
\layout Standard


\size tiny 

\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/erlangDelay.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

State diagram of an Erlang delay system.
\end_inset 


\size default 
The Erlang loss system may be analysed as a birth-death process with birth
 rate 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{k}=\lambda\]

\end_inset 

 and death rate 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mu_{k}=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
k\mu & k=0,1,\dots,m-1\\
m\mu & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 

we derive the detailed balance equations in Example\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:erlangDelayStationary}

\end_inset 

 at the end of the chapter, here we give the result.
 The overall stationary distribution is
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
P\left(0\right)\frac{(m\rho)^{k}}{k!}, & k\leq m\\
P\left(0\right)\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!} & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 

where the normalisation constant is given by
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m-1}\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{k}}{k!}+\frac{\left(m\rho\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}\right]^{-1}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Section

Probability of Waiting
\layout Standard

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue a key quantity of interest is the probability of of a customer queueing,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Q}$
\end_inset 

.
 What is the probability, when the system is at steady state, that a customer
 will arrive and find all the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 servers busy? 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\geq m\right) & = & \sum_{k=m}^{\infty}P\left(k\right)\\
 & = & P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\frac{m^{m}\rho^{k}}{m!}\\
 & = & P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!}\sum_{k=m}^{\infty}\rho^{k-m}\doteq C\left(m,a\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

with a little manipulation (check Example\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:erlangDelayStationary}

\end_inset 

) this expression can be re-written
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
C\left(m,a\right)=P\left(0\right)\frac{\left(\rho m\right)^{m}}{m!\left(1-\rho\right)}.\label{eq:ErlangCone}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

This is known as the 
\emph on 
Erlang C formula
\emph default 
, or Erlang's second formula after A.K.
 Erlang a pioneer of queueing theory.
 By making use of our definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 (from Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:offeredLoad}

\end_inset 

), we can rewrite the offered load as
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}=m\rho\]

\end_inset 

 and substituting into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:ErlangCone}

\end_inset 

) we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
C(m,a)=\frac{P\left(0\right)a^{m}}{m!\left(1-\frac{a}{m}\right)}.\]

\end_inset 

The result of this formula can be computed on a computer: but some care
 must be taken when computing it! For the purposes of exams and exercises
 we will use the old fashioned method of curves.
\layout Section

Erlang C Curves
\layout Standard

We have provided you with two sets of curves in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:erlangCOne}

\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:erlangCTwo}

\end_inset 

.
 In an exam, if they are needed, curves will be provided with the exam paper.
 Make sure you mark the curves and hand them in with your paper.
 To illustrate the use of the curves we will consider a simple telephone
 trunking example and a customer service centre example, see Examples\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:telephoneTrunking}

\end_inset 

\SpecialChar ~
and\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:customerService}

\end_inset 

 at the end of this chapter.
 
