\layout Subsection

Average number in System.
\layout Standard

We can also compute the average number of calls in the Erlang Delay System,
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}C(m,a)+m\rho\]

\end_inset 

 These terms represent the average queue length and the average number of
 busy lines.
 The average queue length is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}C(m,a)\]

\end_inset 

 so using Little's formula the average queueing time is simply 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{N}_{Q}}{\lambda}=\frac{C(m,a)}{m\mu(1-\rho)}.\]

\end_inset 

(see Example\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{example:erlangDelayWait}

\end_inset 

) .
 Note that this is the average queueing time for all customers (whether
 they queued or not).
 It might be more informative to compute the average queueing time for those
 who actually queue.
 This can be easily done using the fact that the average queueing time for
 those who don't queue is zero.
 The average queueing time can be decomposed as
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=C\left(m,a\right)\bar{W}_{Q}+\left(1-C\left(m,a\right)\right)\bar{W}_{\sim Q},\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

 is the average queueing time for those who do queue and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{\sim Q}$
\end_inset 

 is the average queueing time for those who don't queue.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{\sim Q}=0$
\end_inset 

 the second term in this sum is zero, so we can rewrite in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}_{Q}=\frac{\bar{W}}{C\left(m,a\right)}=\frac{1}{m\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}=\frac{1}{\mu\left(m-a\right)}.\]

\end_inset 

Note that the result shows that the average queueing time for those who
 do queue is 
\emph on 
independent 
\emph default 
of the probability of queueing.
