As a result, for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0\dots m$
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right).\label{eq:PkForkLessThanM}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In contrast to the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue, the system state cannot go above 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 so we have no need to consider the region where 
\begin_inset Formula $k>m$
\end_inset 

.
 Substituting in the offered load we have,
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \frac{a}{k}P(k-1),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 which means that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right) & = & \prod_{n=1}^{k}\frac{a}{n}P\left(0\right)\\
 & = & \frac{a^{k}}{k!}P\left(0\right).\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 
\layout Subsection

Normalisation Condition
\layout Standard

The normalisation condition gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}=1\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\frac{1}{\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}},\]

\end_inset 

which leads to the 
\emph on 
truncated Poisson distribution
\emph default 
:
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{k}}{k!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}.\]

\end_inset 

 
\emph on 
Note if 
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula $m\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 


\emph on 
 we recover the
\emph default 
 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 


\emph on 
 queue as we would expect.
\layout Section

Probability of Blocking
\layout Standard

For the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 queue we were interested in the probability that a customer would have
 to wait in the queue for service.
 In the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

 system there is no queue because the maximum state of the system is the
 same as the number of servers.
 The probability of waiting is therefore zero.
 Of more interest is the probability of blocking: the probability that when
 we arrive the system is full and we are denied service.
 As we discussed for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 queue the blocking probability is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset 

, the steady state probability that the system is full.
 This is given as
\begin_inset Formula \[
B(m,a)=P\left(m\right)=\frac{\frac{a^{m}}{m!}}{\sum_{n=0}^{m}\frac{a^{n}}{n!}}.\]

\end_inset 

Once again we would typically need a computer to calculate this quantity,
 but again we will turn to curves for use in exam questions and exercises.
 Erlang B curves plot the blocking probability for different numbers of
 servers as a function of the offered load.
