\layout Section

Carried Load and Utilization Factors
\layout Standard

Since traffic is lost in 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

 the carried load, 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}$
\end_inset 

, is smaller than the offered load.
 The carried load is given by the offered load multiplied by the probability
 that the packet is not blocked.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a^{\prime}=a(1-P\left(m\right))=a(1-B(m,a)).\]

\end_inset 

 The carried load per line is the trunk occupancy, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a^{\prime}}{m},\]

\end_inset 

 also known as the utilization factor.
 Note the contrast with the lossless systems where 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\frac{a}{m}\]

\end_inset 

because the carried load is equal to the offered load 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\prime}=a$
\end_inset 

.
 This also applies for the other system with loss we have seen, the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 Also note that the rate of service in the system is no longer given by
 the arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, we need to consider those packets that are lost.
 This means the rate of service is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\dot{\lambda}=\lambda(1-P\left(m\right))\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset 

 is the blocking probability.
 This has effects when studying the queue using Little's formula.
 When using Little's formula with a lossy system, the rate we use should
 be the service rate, 
\emph on 
not
\emph default 
 the arrival rate.
 Packets which are blocked by the system do not enter it and therefore have
 no effect on the system's state.
