\layout Standard

If service times are 
\emph on 
generally 
\emph default 
distributed we cannot use the birth death process to analyse the system.
 In this chapter we introduce a different type of analysis based on the
 
\emph on 
mean residual service 
\emph default 
time.
 This will allow us to derive the 
\emph on 
Pollaczek-Khinchin 
\emph default 
formula.
 This formula gives the expected queueing time for any distribution of service
 times with 
\emph on 
finite 
\emph default 
variance.
\layout Section

Pollaczek-Khinchin formula
\layout Standard

The Pollaczek-Khinchin formula is given as
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho)}\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{W}$
\end_inset 

 is expected waiting time,
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle _{p(X)}$
\end_inset 

 is the second moment under the service time distribution, the utilisation
 factor is given as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}=\left\langle X\right\rangle _{p(X)}$
\end_inset 

 is the mean under the service time distribution.
 
\layout Section

Proof of Pollaczek-Khinchin Formula
\layout Standard

We will now review a simple proof of the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula.
 In our proof we will make three assumptions:
\layout Enumerate

The queue is FIFO queue, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
customers are served in the order they arrive.
 This assumption may be relaxed but it will simplify our proof.
\layout Enumerate

The arrival times are statistically independent of the service times.
 
\layout Enumerate

The arrival time distribution has a variance which is finite.
\layout Standard

To formulate the proof we introduce a concept known as the mean residual
 service time.
\layout Subsection

The Residual Service Time
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:theResidualServiceTime}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The residual service time is the amount of service time that remains for
 the customer under service from the perspective of a customer who has just
 arrived in the queue.
 The first part of the proof occurs from the perspective of the arriving
 customer.
 The arriving customer is considered to be the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th customer.
 In the table below we introduce some simple definitions of terms.
