\layout Standard

We can use the figure to immediately write down that the wait for the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th customer will be given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
W_{i}=R_{i}+\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 

 summing over both sides (
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
for all the arriving customers up to the 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset 

th) we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{i=1}^{I}W_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{I}R_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{I}\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}X_{j}\]

\end_inset 

dividing through by 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset 

 gives us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\sum_{i=1}^{I}\sum_{j=i-(k_{i}-1)}^{i-1}\frac{X_{j}}{I}.\]

\end_inset 

 In the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $I\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 this becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{W_{i}}{I}=\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{R_{i}}{I}+\lim_{I\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{i=1}^{I}\frac{(k_{i}-1)X_{i}}{I},\]

\end_inset 

the terms of which are recognised as 
\emph on 
time averages
\emph default 
.
 If we assume 
\emph on 
time averages
\emph default 
 are equivalent to 
\emph on 
steady state averages
\emph default 
 this gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle W\right\rangle =\left\langle R\right\rangle +\left\langle \left(k-1\right)X\right\rangle .\]

\end_inset 

Now since the length of the queue as seen by the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival is independent of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

th arrival's length (our second assumption) this means 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle (k-1)X\right\rangle =\left\langle k-1\right\rangle \left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 and since 
\begin_inset Formula $(k-1)$
\end_inset 

 is the length of the queue we may write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\bar{N}_{Q}\bar{X}\]

\end_inset 

 Using Little's theorem for the queue we have
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{Q}=\lambda\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

which gives in turn
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\lambda\bar{X}\bar{W}.\]

\end_inset 

Using the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 (which stems from Little's formula for the packets under service) gives
 us 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\bar{R}+\rho\bar{W}\]

\end_inset 

 Leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho}\label{eqn:arrivals}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 We now have the average queueing time in terms of the mean residual service
 time.
 To complete the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we simply need to calculate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Section

Residual Service Time from Departures Perspective
\layout Standard

We again turn to a graphical proof to obtain the value of the mean residual
 service time.
 In Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:meanResidualGraphical}

\end_inset 

 we present a plot of the residual service time over time.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/residualTime.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Graphical computation of mean residual time.
 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 is the number of service completions in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,\, t]$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:meanResidualGraphical}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The time average of the mean residual service time can then be calculated
 in terms of the area under the curve, 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

, which is given by the area under each of the triangles,
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
A(t)=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}.\]

\end_inset 

 The average service time is the area divided by time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{A(t)}{t} & = & \frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{A(t)}{M(t)}\\
 & = & \frac{1}{2}\frac{M(t)}{t}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M(t)}X_{i}^{2}}{M(t)}.\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

where we have introduced the number of arrivals after time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, as 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, if the variance of the service time distribution is finite, we find that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle .\]

\end_inset 

 Again, assuming that time averages and ensemble averages are equal, we
 substitute back into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eqn:arrivals}

\end_inset 

) to obtain the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho)}.\]

\end_inset 

This implies that the total waiting time in the system is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{X}+\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2(1-\rho)}\]

\end_inset 

 and through Little's formula we can obtain the expected queue length, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}_{Q}$
\end_inset 

, and the expected number in the system, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\series bold 
Note that derivation of this formula was Question 1 in the 2002-2003 paper
\series default 
.
 Now consider Examples\SpecialChar ~

