\layout Section

Automatic Repeat Request (ARQ) System.
 
\layout Standard

The 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue system can be used for modelling various systems of interest.
 In an ARQ system packets are transmitted and the system waits for an acknowledg
ement.
 If no acknowledgement is received the system retransmits.
 In this section we review Example\SpecialChar ~
3.15 from 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

.
 A schematic of the system is given in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:ARQschemeatic}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/ARQ.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

The ARQ System.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:ARQschemeatic}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

For the purposes of this analysis, we will assume 
\layout Enumerate

That each packet takes 1 millisecond to be transmitted.
\layout Enumerate

The system waits a maximum of 
\begin_inset Formula $n-1$
\end_inset 

 milliseconds for an acknowledgement.
 If there is no acknowledgement the system retransmits.
\layout Enumerate

The acknowledgement occurs if the packet is received without errors.
 Otherwise acknowledgement doesn't occur.
 
\layout Enumerate

The probability of an error in a packet is taken to be 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset 

 and we take errors to occur independently.
 
\layout Enumerate

That packets arrive as a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

In practice, the fourth assumption (independence of errors) is probably
 unrealistic .
 If an error occurs due to problems in the transmission channel, it is likely
 that several packets in a row will not be received, so the errors do not
 occur independently.
 The time interval between transmission and re-transmission will be 
\begin_inset Formula $1+kn$
\end_inset 

 milliseconds with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-p\right)p^{k}$
\end_inset 

.
 This time interval is associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 retransmissions and one successful transmission.
 The probability of this being the service time is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X=1+kn\right)=\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\]

\end_inset 

which is recognised as a 
\emph on 
geometric distribution
\emph default 
.
\layout Subsection

Average Service Time
\layout Standard

Using this distribution we can compute the expected service time which is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(1+kn\right)\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\\
 & = & \left(1-p\right)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}+n\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}\right)\\
 & = & 1+n\frac{p}{1-p}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}=\frac{1}{1-p}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}=\frac{p}{\left(1-p\right)^{2}}$
\end_inset 

 .
 
\layout Subsection

ARQ Utilization Factor
\layout Standard

Strictly speaking, it probably doesn't make sense to use the term `utilization
 factor' for the ARQ system.
 This is because during a large portion of the wait it is not actually `utilizin
g' the channel, it is merely waiting for an acknowledgement.
 However we can still consider, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\left\langle X\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

, to be the proportion of time that the system will be `busy'.
 This `busy factor',
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho=\lambda+\lambda n\frac{p}{1-p},\]

\end_inset 

obviously still has to be less than 1, otherwise the steady state would
 not exist.
 This busy time is affected by three parameters: the rate of arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, the amount of time that the system waits for an acknowledgement, 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

, and the probability of an error, 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset 

.
 Note that as 
\begin_inset Formula $p\rightarrow1$
\end_inset 

 the second term 
\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsection

ARQ Second Moment
\layout Standard

The second moment of the service time is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(1+kn\right)^{2}\left(1-p\right)p^{k}\\
 & = & \left(1-p\right)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}p^{k}+2n\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}kp^{k}+n^{2}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k^{2}p^{k}\right)\\
 & = & 1+\frac{2np}{1-p}+\frac{n^{2}\left(p+p^{2}\right)}{\left(1-p\right)^{2}}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

since 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k^{2}p^{k}=\frac{p+p^{2}}{\left(1-p\right)^{3}}\]

\end_inset 

 These moments can be used with the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula to obtain
 statistics for the queue.
\layout Section


\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 Queues with Vacations 
\layout Standard

Consider a server which, when it is free, attends to some other business.
 For example it could transmit control and record keeping traffic.
 These periods are sometimes known as vacations (which is an American word
 that means holidays).
 The vacation periods are characterised by
\layout Enumerate

If a new arrival arrives during a vacation it must wait until the vacation
 is over before being dealt with.
\layout Enumerate

If a vacation ends and the server is idle it immediately goes on a new vacation.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/vacationMG1.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Schematic of 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We denote the length of these successive vacations to be 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dots$
\end_inset 

.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1},$
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula $V_{2}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\dots$
\end_inset 

 are independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables which
 are also independent of the customer times and the service times.
 We can then attempt to compute the mean residual service time for this
 queue for a 
\emph on 
Poisson (Markov) arrival
\emph default 
 system with 
\emph on 
general service times
\emph default 
 and 
\emph on 
generally distributed vacation times
\emph default 
.
\layout Subsection

Residual Service Time
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/vacationResidual.eps
	width 80text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Computation of residual service time for system with vacations.
\end_inset 

For a system with vacations the residual service time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}r\left(\tau\right)d\tau & = & \frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}X_{i}^{2}+\frac{1}{t}\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}\frac{1}{2}V_{i}^{2}\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{M\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{M\left(t\right)}X_{i}^{2}}{M\left(t\right)}+\frac{L\left(t\right)}{t}\frac{1}{2}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{L\left(t\right)}V_{i}^{2}}{L\left(t\right)}\label{eq:forVacationResiduals}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 

where we have introduced the arrival process, 
\begin_inset Formula $M\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

, and this time we have also introduced a process which indicates how many
 vacations there have been, 
\begin_inset Formula $L\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 This means we have to deal with the average number of vacations.
 On average the proportion of time available for vacations is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-\rho\right)$
\end_inset 

, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
that time where the transmission line is not in use.
 If the average length of a vacation at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}_{t}$
\end_inset 

 then the number of vacations that will have arrived at that time is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
L\left(t\right)=\frac{t\left(1-\rho\right)}{\bar{V}_{t}}.\]

\end_inset 

Taking the limit of (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:forVacationResiduals}

\end_inset 

) as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 we can write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{R}=\frac{1}{2}\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle +\frac{\left(1-\rho\right)\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}.\]

\end_inset 

So given that the assumptions that we used to develop the formula 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho}\]

\end_inset 

in Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:theResidualServiceTime}

\end_inset 

 are still valid, we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle }{2\bar{V}}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Examples
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Markov Service times
\series default 
.
 Compute the average waiting time associated with exponentially distributed
 service times using the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{example:MarkovService}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

The exponentially distributed service times, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X\right)=\mu\exp\left(-\mu X\right),\]

\end_inset 

have a mean of 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{X}=\frac{1}{\mu}\]

\end_inset 

 and a variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}$
\end_inset 

 leading to a second moment
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{2}{\mu^{2}}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Substituting into the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{W} & = & \frac{\rho}{\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}\\
 & = & \frac{\rho}{\mu-\lambda}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

which is identical to those we derived for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Deterministic Service times (
\begin_inset Formula $M/D/1$
\end_inset 

)
\series default 
.
 Some systems have deterministic service times.
 For example, transmission of fixed length packets over a channel with a
 fixed bandwidth.
 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{example:DeterministicService}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

We represent a `deterministic distribution' as
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X\right)=\delta\left(X-\frac{1}{\mu}\right)\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\left(\cdot\right)$
\end_inset 

is the Dirac delta function.
 
\layout Standard

For this system 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mu}\]

\end_inset 

and 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mu^{2}}\]

\end_inset 


\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 the variance is zero.
 Substituting into the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\rho}{2\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 
Uniform Service Times
\series default 
 (
\begin_inset Formula $M/U/1$
\end_inset 

).
 Consider a uniform probability distribution is defined between 0 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset 

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $X>0$
\end_inset 

 as
\begin_inset Formula \[
U\left(X|b\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{b} & 0<X\leq b\\
0 & X>b\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 

show that the man of this uniform distribution is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{b}{2}$
\end_inset 

 and its variance by 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{b^{2}}{12}$
\end_inset 

 and find the expected waiting time for an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/U/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{example:uniformService}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

For the mean we have
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}Xp\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}XU\left(X|b\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{2}}{2b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{2b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b}{2}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

For the variance we also require
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle  & = & \int_{0}^{\infty}X^{2}p\left(X\right)dX\\
 & = & \int_{0}^{b}\frac{X^{2}}{b}dX\\
 & = & \left[\frac{X^{3}}{3b}\right]_{0}^{b}\\
 & = & \frac{b^{3}}{3b}-0\\
 & = & \frac{b^{2}}{3}\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle X\right\rangle ^{2}=\frac{b^{2}}{3}-\left(\frac{b}{2}\right)=\frac{b^{2}}{12}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

For simplicity we consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{1}{\left\langle X\right\rangle }.$
\end_inset 

 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=\frac{2}{b}$
\end_inset 

 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\frac{2}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

So we can write the second moment in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{b^{2}}{3}$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Substituting in 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\frac{2}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =\frac{4}{3\mu^{2}}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Substituting in to the Pollaczek-Khinchin formula we recover
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{2\rho}{3\mu\left(1-\rho\right)}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard
}{0}
\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate


\series bold 

\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 with Vacations
\series default 
.
 A bar has Poisson arrivals and one member of staff serving.
 When there are no customers at the bar, the staff member leaves the bar
 to check on the kitchen.
 Assuming these checks are exponentially distributed in length, with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

 and that customer service times are exponentially distributed with mean
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 what is the average waiting time in the queue?
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{example:vacationsExample}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Standard

Exponential standard deviation = 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, mean
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

.
 So if mean is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

, standard deviation is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}$
\end_inset 

 and variance is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Thus second moment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle V^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{V}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Similarly for second moment of service time 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle =2\bar{X}^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 Defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}$
\end_inset 

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\bar{V}.\]

\end_inset 

Deterministic vacations would have lead to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda\bar{X}}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}=\frac{\rho}{\left(1-\rho\right)}+\frac{\bar{V}}{2}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


