\layout Chapter

Networks of Queues and Simulation
\layout Standard

So far in the course we have looked at queueing models in isolation.
 We will now briefly turn to some of the issues that arise when they are
 combined.
 We will start by considering a simple combination of two 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues.
\layout Section


\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 Queues in Tandem
\layout Standard

Consider a simple system involving two transmission lines in tandem.
 The packet length doesn't change during transmission and transmission times
 are proportional to packet length.
 The packet lengths are exponentially distributed and arrivals occur at
 the first queue as a Poisson process.
\layout Standard

The first queue may be modelled as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

, but the second queue cannot be.
 A packet which is large will take a long time in the first queue.
 During this time the second queue will have a chance to clear.
 The result will be that for the second queue larger packets are likely
 to arrive when the queue is smaller.
 This means that a large packet will typically experience less delay at
 the second queue than a small one.
 One analogy for this situation is a slow moving vehicle on a road.
 From this vehicle's driver's perspective there is very little traffic on
 the road because the slow moving vehicle never catches up with any.
 A similar thing occurs with 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues in tandem.
 A large packet arrives at the second queue to find it has less packets
 because the smaller packets have had time to be processed.
 The effect of the first queue is to destroy the independence of the packet
 length and the state of the second queue.
 This problem is difficult to deal with analytically.
 To proceed with our analysis we will need to break this dependency.
 This can be achieved by considering service times for each packet to be
 statistically independent at each queue.
 We will now look at the consequences of this assumption for our simple
 pairing of two queues, but first we must introduce the concept of time
 reversibility.
\layout Section

Time Reversibility --- Burke's Theorem
\layout Standard

In the analysis of the Markov chain systems we have seen so far (
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1/m$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m/m$
\end_inset 

) we relied on detailed balance to find the steady state distribution.
 Detailed balance equations are a special case of global balance equations
 which arise when transitions only occur between neighbouring states in
 the Markov chain.
 Detailed balance can be applied to find the stationary distribution in
 any birth-death processes.
\layout Standard

In this section we will briefly mention an additional special property of
 birth-death processes (and other Markov systems for which detailed balance
 applies) known as 
\emph on 
time reversibility.

\emph default 
 The principle with time reversibility is as follows: for a birth-death
 process at steady state the arrival process is Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 Time reversibility states that the departure process will also be Poisson
 with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

We will now give some motivation for this statement, a proof is Given in
 Section 3.7 of 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

.
 Consider the following `intuition' for the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 The service rate for the system is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 However this is not the departure rate because a departure can only occur
 when the system has a state 
\begin_inset Formula $k>0$
\end_inset 

.
 This condition is satisfied for a fraction of the time which is given by
 the utilization factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

.
 the departure rate is therefore given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\rho=\mu\frac{\lambda}{\mu}=\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Section

Burke's Theorem
\layout Standard

Burke's theorem follows on from time reversibility.
 It states that if an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

, an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/m$
\end_inset 

 or an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/\infty$
\end_inset 

 queue
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

Queues with loss are slightly more complex because the arrival process does
 not have rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 due to blocked packets.
\end_inset 

 at its stationary distribution has a Poisson arrival process with a rate
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 then the departure process is also Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 Furthermore the number of the customers in the system at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is 
\emph on 
independent 
\emph default 
of the sequence of the departure times prior to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

The second property is worthy of comment as it sounds counterintuitive.
 You might expect that if there were a rapid series of departures from the
 queue then the queue would be shorter than normal, but Burke's theorem
 tells us that this is not the case.
 From this we can infer that the state of a queue 
\emph on 
before 
\emph default 
a rapid series of departures is about to occur must be abnormally high.
 Otherwise it 
\emph on 
would
\emph default 
 be abnormally low after the departures, but Burke's theorem tells us that
 this is not the case.
\layout Section


\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 Queues in Tandem Revisited
\layout Standard

Let's return to the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues in tandem.
 Burke's theorem tells us that the departure process from the first queue
 will be a Poisson with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda.$
\end_inset 

Now if we allow service times at the second queue to be independent of service
 times at the first queue we can break the dependence of the queue length
 at the second queue on the service time at the second queue.
 In this case the system can be modelled as two 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues.
 So if the service time distributions at the two queues are governed by
 exponential distributions with rate parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{2}$
\end_inset 

 leading to utilization factors for each queue as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{1}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu_{1}}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{2}=\frac{\lambda}{\mu_{2}}$
\end_inset 

.
 then the distributions for the states of the systems are given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k_{1}\right)=\rho_{1}^{k_{1}}\left(1-\rho_{1}\right)\]

\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k_{2}\right)=\rho_{2}^{k_{2}}\left(1-\rho_{2}\right).\]

\end_inset 

The joint distribution for both systems is then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k_{1},k_{2}\right) & = & P\left(k_{1}\right)P\left(k_{2}\right)\\
 &  & \rho_{1}^{k_{1}}\left(1-\rho_{1}\right)\rho_{2}^{k_{2}}\left(1-\rho_{2}\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

because the two queues can be considered independently.
 As a result the average delay in the system can be computed as the sum
 of the average delays in each queue,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\bar{T}_{1}+\bar{T}_{2}=\frac{1}{\mu_{1}-\lambda}+\frac{1}{\mu_{2}-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Section

Kleinrock's Independence Approximation
\layout Standard

Kleinrock's independence approximation suggests assuming independence for
 every node in the network.
 The approximation is good if large numbers of packets from outside the
 network are introduced at every queue.
 
\layout Standard

Consider a general network with labelled nodes and links between these nodes.
 There are several packet streams in the network, each stream has a rate
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}$
\end_inset 

.
 We define 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{ij}\left(s\right)$
\end_inset 

 as the fraction of packets from stream 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

 that cross the link between 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

 and j.
 The rate associated with a link between node 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset 

and 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset 

j is then 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda_{ij}=\sum_{s}f_{ij}\left(s\right)x_{s}.\]

\end_inset 

Kleinrock suggested that merging traffic streams on a transmission line
 has the effect of restoring independence of arrival and service times.
 The approach is to assume that each link can be treated as an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 This is a good approximation for:
\layout Enumerate

Arrivals which occur as a Poisson process.
\layout Enumerate

Exponentially distributed packets.
\layout Enumerate

Densely connected networks.
\layout Enumerate

Moderate to heavy traffic.
\layout Standard

Given this assumption, from the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue, we know that the average number at each link is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{ij}=\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}}\]

\end_inset 

thus the average number in the entire system is
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\bar{N} & = & \sum_{ij}\bar{N}_{ij}\\
 & = & \sum_{ij}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}},\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

average delay at each link is given by Little's theorem
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}_{ij}=\frac{1}{\mu_{ij}+\lambda_{ij}}.\]

\end_inset 

In our studies we will assume that there is no further delay associated
 with each link (propagation, processing).
 In practice such delays may arise however we shall not consider them.
 See 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

 Section 3.6.1 for more details.
\layout Section

Jackson's Theorem
\layout Standard

While Kleinrock's independence assumption approximates the activities at
 each queue as 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queues, Jackson's theorem provides a result for the number in the queueing
 system as a whole.
 Some analysis of the system is given in Section 3.8 of 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

, but for our purposes it is sufficient to give the assumptions and the
 result.
 The first assumption is the premise of Kleinrock's independence assumption:
 there is no correlation between service times and queue lengths.
 The second assumption is that randomisation is used to divide the traffic
 along different routes through the network.
 Given these assumptions the average number of packets in the network is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\sum_{i,j}\frac{\lambda_{ij}}{\mu_{ij}-\lambda_{ij}}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Section

Network Simulation
\layout Standard

The approximations we have outlined so far may be useful for `back of the
 envelope' style calculations.
 In practice though it is highly likely that you will wish to simulate the
 system.
 There are a range of simulation packages available, we will introduce a
 simple one based on the Tcl scripting language called `The Network Simulator'
 or ns-2 (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{http://www.isi.edu/nsnam/ns/}

\end_inset 

).
 It is a discrete event simulator aimed at networking research.
 It supports a range of protocols including TCP and multicast protocols
 and is straightforward to extend using C++.
 It is a powerful research tool that has the added advantage of being freely
 available.
 It is installed in the Lewin Laboratory on the Linux systems and, if you
 require a personal installation, it can be installed through the Cygwin
 API (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \url{http://www.cygwin.com}

\end_inset 

).
\layout Section

Network Simulation with NS-2
\layout Standard

At the second lab class you will simulate some simple queues in NS2 and
 compare the simulations with approximate results obtained using Kleinrock's
 independence assumption.
 The lab class will be completed in Linux so please make sure you know your
 Unix password for the DCS system before the lab starts.
 The lab sheet has some notes on simple commands you will need.
 If you are unfamiliar with Linux it is suggested that you try them out
 before starting the lab.
