\layout Chapter

Multiaccess Systems
\layout Standard

The final topic we will cover is a brief review of multiaccess systems.
 These are systems where there is one communication channel and multiple
 stations (or nodes) making use of it.
 Examples of multiaccess systems include Satellite systems, where one Satellite
 serves many ground stations; packet radio networks, where many stations
 communicate across the same radio bandwidths; a multitapped bus, where
 several computers are attached to the same transmission line.
\layout Standard

A very common example of multi-access channels is the local area network.
 In a local area network transmission rates are around 10 Mbps to 1Gbps.
 Transmission distances range from a few meters to several kilometres.
 The required error rates are low: typically from 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-8}$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $10^{-11}$
\end_inset 

, that's about one error in every Gigabyte.
\layout Standard

Typically we are interested in the throughput of the network --- the amount
 of data or messages passing through the network each second.
 If the number of overhead bits (associated with network administration
 
\emph on 
etc.
\emph default 
) is low then this is approximately equal to the utilization factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

Until the introduction of the ALOHA network the standard approach to splitting
 a channel was through multiplexing.
 We briefly review multiplexing in the examples section at the end of this
 chapter, recreating Example 3.9 from 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Section

Channel Multiplexing
\layout Standard

Let's assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 statistically independent Poisson streams.
 Each has arrival rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\lambda}{m}$
\end_inset 

.
 The packets to be transmitted are assumed to have an exponentially distributed
 length which, for a given bandwidth leads to a service time distribution
 for the whole transmission line which is an exponential with a rate parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsection

Statistical Multiplexing
\layout Standard

If we merge the Poisson streams together, through statistical multiplexing
 the superposition property of the Poisson distribution (from Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:superpositionPoisson}

\end_inset 

) tells us that they become a Poisson stream with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 If the transmission times are exponentially distributed the average delay,
 from the 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue, will be 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Time or Frequency Division Multiplexing
\layout Standard

With the alternative approach of providing each communication channel with
 a separate circuit the transmission line needs to be split into 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 separate channels.
 Each of these separate channels will now have service time distributions
 with a reduced rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mu}{m}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Delay in this system will now be 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 times larger than that for statistical multiplexing
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{T}=\frac{1}{\frac{\mu}{m}-\frac{\lambda}{m}}=\frac{m}{\mu-\lambda}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Why does this happen?
\layout Standard

The reduction in delay arises from the fact that when only one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 channels is transmitting with statistical multiplexing it can use the entire
 bandwidth of the channel.
 With time or frequency multiplexing 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{m-1}{m}$
\end_inset 

 of the bandwidth is wasted.
\layout Section

Multiaccess Systems
\layout Standard

In multiaccess systems splitting the channel using time division multiplexing
 is known as time division multiple access (TDMA).
 Similarly splitting the channel using frequency division multiplexing is
 known as frequency division multiple access (FDMA).
 A further technique is known as code division multiple access (CDMA) but
 we will not discuss that here.
\layout Standard

We have already shown that statistical multiplexing can make more efficient
 use of bandwidth than time or frequency based multiplexing.
 However in a multiaccess system it can be difficult to implement in the
 manner discussed above.
 We assumed that all the transmissions could be queued in a single 
\begin_inset Formula $M/M/1$
\end_inset 

 queue.
 In a local area network the transmitting stations (or nodes) are distributed
 across different portions of the network as are the receiving nodes.
 Forming a single queue is therefore not possible without some form of centralis
ed control.
 TDMA and FDMA can be implanted by dedicating one channel for each station
 to transmit on, but allowing each station to receive on any channel.
 This then allows for an analysis of the type we described above.
 However, it is not clear how to perform statistical multiplexing in these
 circumstances.
\layout Section

Random Access
\layout Standard

One way of performing statistical multiplexing when we have stations distributed
 across a single communication channel would be to have a centralised computer
 attempting to control which computer is able to access our transmission
 lines at what times.
 However, we may wish communication to occur without centralised control.
 In this case we may choose a random access solution.
\layout Standard

We will consider random access networks where there is one communication
 channel on which all the nodes communicate
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

It could be the case that only a subset of the nodes have access to the
 channel.
 For example in a packet radio network the range between stations may mean
 that only a subset of nodes are able to receive from any particular node.
 However this complicates analysis so we will ignore this possibility.
\end_inset 

.
 Each station sends a packet immediately whenever it receives a packet to
 send.
 this means that packets can 
\emph on 
collide
\emph default 
 and may have to be resent.
\layout Subsection

Pure ALOHA Networks
\layout Standard

The ALOHA network was developed at the University of Hawaii, it was a packet
 radio network which communicated between different stations on the Hawaiian
 islands.
 For the ALOHA network (and Ethernet, the modern LAN protocol that was inspired
 by ALOHA) all stations attempt to communicate on the same channel.
 When a station attempts to transmit, it detects if its transmission has
 interfered with an existing transmission: this is known as a collision.
 If a collision occurs the station becomes 
\emph on 
backlogged 
\emph default 
and attempts to transmit at a later time.
 If both stations attempted to retransmit after the 
\emph on 
same 
\emph default 
period of time they would simply collide again after retransmission.
 To avoid this problem the stations normally wait for a random period of
 time before retransmitting.
\layout Standard

For this network we assume that each station, after a given propagation
 delay, can tell whether the transmitted packets were correctly received.
 Furthermore, we assume that each packet requires 1 `time unit' for transmission.
 This means that if a packet of interest is sent at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, then any other packet sent between time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 will collide with our packet.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/pureALOHA.eps
	width 100text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Pure ALOHA, packets collide with any other packet transmitted within one
 time unit of their transmission.
 The diagram shows a packet sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 and packets sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 Any packet sent after 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

 and before 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset 

 will collide with the packet sent at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We will now look at a simple analysis of the ALOHA system.
 Let's assume that the total arrival rate for packets at all stations is
 a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 We assume that the number of stations is very large (infinite) so that
 an individual station's packet rate is an insignificant proportion of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, 
\emph on 
i.e.
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\lambda_{n}}{\lambda}=\lim_{\delta\rightarrow0}=0.\]

\end_inset 

A station is considered backlogged between the time that it became aware
 that its previous transmission failed and the time of a successful transmission.
 We denote the number of backlogged stations at any particular time as 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

.
 Once a station is aware that a transmission was unsuccessful it waits for
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}$
\end_inset 

 time units to retransmit.
 The waiting time is sampled from a probability density function given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(T_{i}\right)=\mu\exp\left(-\mu T_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 

So if 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 nodes are backlogged then (from superposition of Poissons) the arrivals
 from the backlogged nodes are a Poisson process with rate
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

In 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \citeauthor{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

 they use 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 as the parameter instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula $n\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 So the overall arrival process, from the perspective of the channel, is
 Poisson with a rate that is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)=\lambda+n\mu$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

The probability that there are no arrivals in an interval of 2 time units
 for a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k=0|G\left(n\right),2\right)=\exp\left(-2G\left(n\right)\right)\]

\end_inset 

as we derived in Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sec:poissonProcess}

\end_inset 

.
 This is the probability that there is no collision between the packet of
 interest and any other packets in the network, thus it is the probability
 of a successful transmission.
 The rate of attempted transmissions is 
\begin_inset Formula $G\left(n\right)$
\end_inset 

 so the rate of successful transmissions is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}\left(n\right)=G\left(n\right)\exp\left(-2G\left(n\right)\right),\]

\end_inset 

this is known as the throughput.
 It is the number of packets that successfully reach their destination.
 The throughput is plotted as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:pureALOHAThroughput}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/pureALOHAThroughput.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Throughput for pure ALOHA as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:pureALOHAThroughput}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Note that the curve goes up to a maximum point and drops down thereafter.
 We can find the maximum of the throughput by looking for points where the
 gradient of the throughput is zero.
 In the examples below we find that the maximum throughput for the system
 occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 The throughput at this point is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2e}\approx0.184$
\end_inset 

.
 This maximum throughput shows that at best the channel is in use only about
 18% of the time
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

Note that since the service time is fixed at 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}=1$
\end_inset 

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 then the utilization for this system is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\lambda\bar{X}=\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 

.
 The system will be in equilibrium if the throughput equals 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda.$
\end_inset 

 In other words if the throughput is equal to the arrival rate of the packets.
\layout Subsection

Slotted ALOHA
\layout Standard

A simple improvement to the ALOHA system comes from constraining when packets
 can be sent.
 Instead of allowing stations to transmit immediately they receive a packet
 each station waits for the beginning of a `slot' to start transmitting.
 These slots are synchronised across the entire network.
 In this system a collision occurs if another station attempts to transmit
 in the same slot.
 This happens if another station received a packet for transmission in the
 preceding slot.
 If each slot is one time unit in length the probability of this event is
 given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(k=0|G\left(n\right),2\right)=\exp\left(-G\left(n\right)\right).\]

\end_inset 

The probability is smaller than for pure ALOHA because the `vulnerable period'
 for a collision is smaller (half the length of before).
 A collision now only occurs if another station attempts to transmit in
 the same slot.
 As a result the rate of successful transmissions is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}\left(n\right)=G\left(n\right)\exp\left(-G\left(n\right)\right),\]

\end_inset 

this is known as the throughput.
 It is the number of packets that successfully reach their destination.
 The throughput is plotted as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:slottedALOHAThroughput}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

The smaller vulnerable period leads to a greater maximum throughput which
 is now found at 
\begin_inset Formula $G=1$
\end_inset 

.
 The maximum throughput is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{e}$
\end_inset 

.
 Slotted ALOHA therefore can be twice as efficient as pure ALOHA.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/slottedALOHAThroughput.eps
	width 60text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Throughput for slotted ALOHA as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:slottedALOHAThroughput}

