Answer
\layout Standard

To find stationary points we seek the gradient of the throughput.
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}=G\exp\left(-2G\right)\]

\end_inset 

differentiating with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset 

 we obtain
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{dG^{\prime}}{dG} & = & \exp\left(-2G\right)-2G\exp\left(-2G\right)\\
 & = & \left(1-2G\right)\exp\left(-2G\right)\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

to find stationary points we solve for points where the gradient is zero,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(1-2G\right)\exp\left(-2G\right)=0\]

\end_inset 

which implies that either 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp\left(-2G\right)=0$
\end_inset 

, this happens when 
\begin_inset Formula $G\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G^{\prime}\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

; or 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1-2G\right)=0$
\end_inset 

 which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 The first of these stationary points is a minimum, the second is a maximum.
 To find the value of the maximum we insert 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset 

 into our throughput equation giving
\begin_inset Formula \[
G^{\prime}=\frac{1}{2}\exp\left(-1\right)\approx0.184.\]

\end_inset 


